<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suffolk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suffolk is a county of East Anglia in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a> as its county town. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> its land was divided between the Iceni and the Trinovantes, and from the 5th century it was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and formed the southern part of the kingdom of East Anglia; its name means 'south folk'. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> royal burial ground of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suffolk.jpg" alt="Suffolk" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Komarov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orford_Ness_2016-08-27-2_(cropped,_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suffolk is a county of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in East Anglia, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to the east, Essex to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a> to the west. Its coast has several deep estuaries, including those of the Blyth, Deben, Orwell, Stour and Alde, and a large spit at Orford Ness. Inland the landscape is flat and gently undulating, and the county is largely arable farmland; its highest point is Great Wood Hill, at 128 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1797 John Frere found flint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-axe">hand axes</a> in a deposit twelve feet deep at Hoxne and suggested that they belonged to a very remote period, the earliest recognition that hand axes were made by early humans. At the start of the Roman period most of Suffolk was inhabited by the Iceni, with the Trinobantes in parts of the south. The Iceni unsuccessfully revolted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> in 60–61 CE. Anglian settlement followed in the 5th and 6th centuries, and the area was mostly governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a>. Sutton Hoo, an elaborate royal ship burial, is probably that of King Rædwald, whose son Sigeberht Christianised East Anglia.</p><p>In the medieval period Suffolk became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> incursions, and the county was formed in the 10th or 11th century. After 1066 castles were built at Framlingham, Clare and Eye, and the abbey at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bury-st-edmunds">Bury St Edmunds</a> became one of the wealthiest religious institutions in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. King Edmund of East Anglia, after whom Bury is named, was killed by invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> in 869. Towns such as Lavenham and Hadleigh prospered from the cloth and wool trade in the 15th and 16th centuries, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> the county was a centre of Puritan and parliamentary strength.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suffolk is a ceremonial county whose county town and largest settlement is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a>. Its other principal towns include Lowestoft, Haverhill and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bury-st-edmunds">Bury St Edmunds</a>. Felixstowe is a major container port, and Newmarket is the headquarters of British horseracing. The Hoxne Hoard, found in 1992, is the largest assembly of late Roman silver and gold found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> treasures include a sword of state, a helmet and a lyre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suffolk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Little Domesday, physically smaller but more detailed, covers Norfolk, <b>Suffolk</b> and Essex and records the livestock on lords' home farms.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2014 the statue was moved to make way for the Luas tram tracks, repaired by Bushy Park Ironworks, and set up in July outside the Dublin Tourist Office on <b>Suffolk</b> Street, formerly St. Andrew's Church, where some walking tours of the city start and end.</blockquote><a href="/songs/molly-malone">Molly Malone</a></li><li><blockquote>On the second day of the Battle of Jargeau, Joan of Arc led the French army in taking the city and capturing the English commander, William de la Pole, 1st Duke of <b>Suffolk</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Danish settlers took land across Norfolk and <b>Suffolk</b>, and soon after his death coins honouring the martyred King Edmund were issued, showing rapid integration.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-kingdom-of-east-anglia">Danish Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iceni held Norfolk and parts of <b>Suffolk</b> and Cambridgeshire; they are probably the Cenimagni who surrendered to Julius Caesar in 54 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/iceni">Iceni</a></li><li><blockquote>Angles settled Norfolk and <b>Suffolk</b> in the 5th century, and the Wuffingas dynasty, named after Wuffa, ruled by the 6th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
